{
  "dom_id": "step-q332120",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14182807/assets/ba6fb6239cc146b1fb5fc31300015984826e4ab1fe4061a0bd14ce3b2643569f.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L\\{{e}^{t}f(t)\\}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $1$ unit to the right.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14182807/assets/97b2ac07998dea2ba0116ae4b1b26bccdc2c6b2a64d99836578c01d5502c61d2.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
